# Convex-position capacity and the necessary global hull change

**Date:** 2026-10-12  
**Base commit:** `13093c032a8d6a7a929c42225b9972e6429bd72c`  
**Canonical base:** `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`  
**State version:** `2026-10-10.audit.234`  
**Target:** independent full-source Level I competitor route  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; regression control  
**Level I:** OPEN. The arbitrary global-minimizer replacement is not constructed.

## 1. Global endpoint and scope

```text
arbitrary sufficiently large triangle-free global minimizer
  -> unrestricted full-source strict-sub-D replacement
  -> contradiction -> Level I
```

This report studies what an unrestricted competitor must change, rather than
fixing a contact phase or adding parameters to an affine motion. It proves a
global barrier to preserving convex position, even when the old radii, angles,
contacts, and entire interior population may all change.

For a finite unit-separated set X, let c(X) be the largest cardinality of a subset
in convex position: every point of the subset must be a vertex of its own convex
hull. The subset need not lie on the boundary of the full source hull. Source
labels and contact isolates are always included in the full replacement.

## 2. The classical finite polygon perimeter inequality, with a proof

For a convex k-gon P of diameter d and perimeter p,

\[
\boxed{p\le2kd\sin(\pi/(2k)).}
\tag{1}
\]

This is the Reinhardt--Datta perimeter inequality. No novelty is claimed for (1).
The normalization and all-k upper bound were checked in Christian Bingane,
*Tight bounds on the maximal perimeter and the maximal width of convex small
polygons*, Theorem 2 and the introduction, pp. 1 and 4 of the
[author's manuscript](https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/8053-1.pdf).
The original Datta paper is *A Discrete Isoperimetric Problem*, Geometriae Dedicata
64, 55--68 (1997), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1004997002327).
The following direct proof avoids importing any equality or uniqueness assertion.

**Proof.** The difference body K=P+(-P) is centrally symmetric about zero,
has at most 2k sides, and lies in the closed radius-d disk. Its boundary edge
directions are obtained by merging the cyclic direction lists of P and -P.
Parallel edges merge by adding their lengths. Hence

\[
\operatorname{per}(K)=2p.
\]

Let v_1,...,v_l be K's vertices, l<=2k. Radially extend each to the radius-d circle,
writing w_i=d v_i/|v_i|. Their convex hull contains zero, since the directions are
centrally symmetric, and hence contains every old v_i on [0,w_i]. Perimeter is
monotone under convex inclusion. If gamma_i are the cyclic angular gaps of the
w_i, their sum is 2pi and the circumscribed vertex polygon has perimeter

\[
2d\sum_{i=1}^l\sin(\gamma_i/2)
\le2ld\sin(\pi/l)
\le4kd\sin(\pi/(2k)).
\]

The first inequality is Jensen's inequality for sine on [0,pi]. The second uses
the increase of x sin(pi/x) for x>=2; its derivative is sin t-t cos t>0 for
0<t<=pi/2. Dividing by two proves (1).

For completeness, perimeter monotonicity here is elementary. For a convex polygon
with edge vectors e and projection width w(theta), traversing its boundary gives
sum_e |e dot u(theta)|=2w(theta). Integrating from zero to pi gives
per(P)=integral_0^pi w(theta) dtheta. Convex inclusion increases every width. ∎

## 3. A label-independent convex-position capacity

**Theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** Every finite unit-separated
planar source X of diameter d>=1 satisfies

\[
\boxed{c(X)\le\left\lfloor{\pi\over2\arcsin(1/(2d))}\right\rfloor.}
\tag{2}
\]

**Proof.** If a subset of size k>=3 is in convex position, each of its k hull sides
has length at least one. Its perimeter is at least k and its diameter is at most d.
Equation (1) gives 1<=2d sin(pi/(2k)), which rearranges to (2). Subsets of size two
are covered because the right side is at least three for d>=1. ∎

The hypothesis is arbitrary unit-separated source geometry; neither triangle-freeness,
globality, KKT, full support, contact minimality, nor lattice coherence is assumed.
The bound concerns every convex subset, not just the actual hull vertices.

## 4. A barrier to all convex boundary rearrangements

For odd q>=5 set

\[
R_q={1\over2\sin(\pi/q)},\qquad
d_q={1\over2\sin(\pi/(2q))}=2R_q\cos(\pi/(2q)).
\]

A regular unit q-gon has circumradius R_q and diameter d_q.

**Theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** Suppose a finite unit-separated
source A contains those q rim vertices and has full diameter exactly d_q. Then
c(A)=q. Every unit-separated full-source replacement B of strictly smaller diameter
has c(B)<=q-1. In particular, under every bijective label assignment, the images of
the q old rim labels fail to be in convex position.

**Proof.** The rim gives c(A)>=q, while (2) at d=d_q gives c(A)<=q. For d<d_q,
pi/(2 arcsin(1/(2d)))<q, so (2) gives c(B)<=q-1. Alternatively, if the old rim images
remained q vertices of their own convex hull, (1) and their unit separation would
force diam(B)>=d_q. ∎

All other source points may move arbitrarily or be replaced outright at the same
cardinality. The old rim need not remain on a circle, preserve its cyclic order,
keep its old edges unit, or be the full hull of B. Preserving convex position alone
already blocks strict improvement. Conversely, losing convex position is necessary
only; it does not check any lower constraint or construct a feasible competitor.

The radial-multiset obstruction in the unreviewed
[October 11 report at frozen commit d22fce8b](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/blob/d22fce8b03b34b735170e92018340ee23d4c3320/docs/reports/global_radial_reassignment_and_dense_local_trap_2026-10-11.md) has a narrower
invariant. That report's independent positive-stress, mixed-rank, dense local-trap,
and exact 175-label results are not imported here. This report makes no priority
claim for local minimality of regular odd rims.

## 5. Quantitative displacement barrier, even with unrestricted interior moves

**Corollary (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** With A as above, assign its
labels bijectively to a unit-separated B of smaller diameter. For every common
Euclidean isometry used to align B with A,

\[
\boxed{\max_{i\text{ an old rim label}}|b_i-p_i|
\ge{\sin(\pi/q)\over2}={1\over4R_q}.}
\tag{3}
\]

No displacement bound is imposed on any interior label.

**Proof.** In radial unit direction u_i=p_i/R_q, the old vertex p_i exceeds every
other rim vertex's projection by at least

\[
R_q(1-\cos(2\pi/q))=\sin(\pi/q)=g.
\]

If every rim displacement were less than g/2, its new projection would exceed every
other new rim projection in that same direction: each of the two projections changes
by less than g/2. Each b_i would remain uniquely exposed in conv({b_j: old rim labels}),
contradicting Section 4. The same reasoning applies after every alignment and every
label assignment. ∎

This supplies a local basin under arbitrary collective interior changes and a
source-wide lower bound on the necessary boundary transport. The scale is O(1/d_q),
so it does not give a uniform large displacement or a macroscopic energy barrier.

## 6. Binding exact control: the canonical heptagon escape

The canonical regression `C-HEPTAGON-PSD-STRESS-FINITE-COMPETITOR-REGRESSION` has a
regular unit heptagon as its losing source. Its strict contact-preserving replacement
is a unit hexagon and its center, with labeled coordinates

\[
(0,0),(1,0),(1/2,\sqrt3/2),(-1/2,\sqrt3/2),
(-1,0),(-1/2,-\sqrt3/2),(1/2,-\sqrt3/2).
\]

All 21 pair distances are at least one; squared diameter is four. The old seven-cycle
contacts remain unit. The center is the average of labels 1,3,5, so all seven labels
are no longer in convex position, while the six outer points are. Thus c(B)=6, exactly
consistent with Section 4. This is a nonglobal source, and its replacement adds contacts
and triangles. The new theorem does not conflict with the canonical regression.

## 7. Verification, dependencies, and the global gap

`python3 research/verify_global_convex_position_barrier_2026_10_12.py` checks the
difference-body edge/perimeter identities on exact rational polygons, the symbolic
radial support-gap identity, and all pair distances, old contacts, convex position,
and center barycenter of the heptagon replacement. The universal polygon bound and
all-source statements are proved analytically above; samples are not exhaustion.

The load-bearing dependencies are the elementary difference-body/Jensen proof and
the exact definition of global optimality. The canonical heptagon regression is a
control, not a proof premise. The literature transcription checks normalization and
priority, not an additional unproved hypothesis. No canonical state is changed.

